### Using `process` (for simple input)

For simple command-line input, you can use `process.stdin` to read from the standard input stream.

```ts
// example.ts
const { stdin } from 'node:process';

// Read input from the command line
stdin.on('data', (data: string) => {
  console.log(`You entered: ${data}`);
});

stdin.on('end', () => {
  console.log('End of input.');
});
```

### Using `readline` (for better control)

The `readline` module provides a more convenient way to read user input line by line.

```ts
// example.ts
import { createInterface } from 'node:readline';

const rl = createInterface({
  input: process.stdin,
  output: process.stdout
});

rl.on('line', (line: string) => {
  console.log(`You entered: ${line}`);
});

rl.on('close', () => {
  console.log('End of input.');
});
```

### Using `readline-sync` (for synchronous input)

If you want to read input synchronously (i.e., block the program until input is received), you can use the `readline-sync` package.

